      'Some architectural historians have grown cautious about \'solar\' as a term because it was heavily popularized by nineteenth-century antiquarians constructing a somewhat romanticized picture of castle life, while the actual medieval documentary record more often just uses plain functional language like \'chamber\' or \'great chamber\' for the same space, without necessarily implying the sunlit-room etymology sometimes assumed. This is a smaller-scale version of a recurring problem in castle studies, where confident-sounding period terminology in guidebooks and even older scholarship turns out to rest more on later convention than on close reading of primary sources. It doesn\'t mean \'solar\' is simply wrong, since it remains a widely understood shorthand, but careful modern scholarship increasingly flags it as needing qualification rather than treating it as a settled medieval technical term. A related detail: similar terminological caution is now applied to \'keep,\' a term rarely used in medieval sources themselves, which more often called the same structure a \'donjon\' or simply \'the tower.\'',
